chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 22 of the 3rd edition, Recognizing Text in Images, defines optical character recognition and teaches the PyTesseract package with the Tesseract engine: installing the engine on Windows, macOS and Linux, the four lines that open an image with Pillow and pass it to image_to_string(), the flaws of OCR text and the preprocessing that reduces them, correction of the text with a large language model, the lang argument and get_languages() for non-English text, and the NAPS2 application run through subprocess to build a PDF with embedded text; it closes with 7 practice questions and the practice program Browser Text Scraper." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's four lines run under Python 3.14.4 with current packages: with PyTesseract 0.3.13 (released in August 2024, for Python 3.8 and later), Pillow 12.3.0 and Tesseract 5.3.4 a rendered line of text comes back as 'Hello OCR world 2026' followed by a newline, the function takes a Pillow image or a file path with the same result, PyTesseract declares Pillow as a requirement so installing it installs Pillow, and its result equals what the tesseract program itself prints. The book's own images (ocr-example.png, frankenstein.png, frankenstein_jpn.png) are served from a resources page that refused this study with an access error, so the chapter's printed OCR results were not reproduced; only text rendered for this study was read." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P04 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's list of flaws and of preprocessing steps agrees with the Tesseract documentation, which adds that dark text on a light background is advice for version 4 and later, that a missing border and a border that is too large both cause trouble, that the default page mode expects a page of text so a small region may need --psm, and that Tesseract binarises internally and since 5.0.0 also offers Adaptive Otsu and Sauvola. Executed on rendered text with Tesseract 5.3.4: a line cropped tight to its letters gave an empty string until a 10-pixel white border or --psm 7 was added, and --psm 7 read the C of OCR as G; a slant of 12 degrees gave an empty string, 8 degrees misread one letter and 5 degrees read correctly; two columns were read across the page as one line each; and white text on a black background was read correctly, where the documentation advises dark text for version 4." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P04, chx:P05 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's claim that Tesseract assumes English holds: the lang parameter of image_to_string() has the default None in the signature and the documentation says the language defaults to eng. get_languages() returned ['eng', 'osd'] on the machine of this study, not the long list the chapter prints, because only English and orientation data are installed. A language whose pack is missing raises TesseractError (a RuntimeError) for lang='jpn', but lang='eng+jpn' raised nothing: the program wrote a warning to standard error, exited with status 0 and read with English alone, so a missing pack beside a present one can go unnoticed. Codes are mostly three letters with exceptions such as aze_cyrl, and ISO 639-2 gives some languages two codes (fre and fra, ger and deu), of which the chapter's fra and deu are the ones Tesseract uses." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P06, chx:P13, chx:P14 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's NAPS2 call is the documented one: -i imports, -o names the output, -n 0 scans nothing, -f overwrites, -v prints progress, --install ocr-eng installs a pack and --ocrlang sets the language and implies --enableocr; the documented slice notation starts at 0 and accepts [0], [-1], [:2], [2:] and [2:-2] with the meaning of Python slices, as the chapter says. The documentation adds --disableocr, --deskew and --rotate, that imported files are prepended to the output in the order given, and deb and rpm installers for Linux with the command naps2 console besides the Flatpak the chapter names; the download page lists release 8.3.2. The OCRmyPDF package the chapter recommends as an alternative adds an OCR text layer to existing PDF files. Neither NAPS2 nor OCRmyPDF is installed on the machine of this study, so no call to them was run." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P07, chx:P08, chx:P09 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter calls subprocess.run() and does not look at the result; the documentation describes the returned CompletedProcess, and with check=True a non-zero exit status raises CalledProcessError. Executed with a stand-in program, since NAPS2 is not installed: the arguments of a list reach the program unsplit ('my scan.png' stays one argument), the exit status 3 is in returncode, check=True raises CalledProcessError, and a program that is not found raises FileNotFoundError. The practice program appends with open() in mode 'a', whose encoding is the locale's unless encoding is given, and with -X warn_default_encoding an EncodingWarning is issued, so text that may hold non-ASCII characters is appended with encoding='utf-8'; run twice, the appending program kept both pages of recognised text in one file." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P11, chx:P12 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's correction with a large language model was not executed, since it needs a network service and an account. What a program can do for itself was executed: a dictionary test accepts 'modem' where the text should read 'modern', so a spellcheck finds the wrong kind of mistake; a regular expression removes an end-of-line hyphen but joins 'half-' and 'extinguished' into one word although the hyphen belongs to the word; and the words that differ between the raw and the corrected text can be listed and scored, which gives the human reviewer the chapter asks for what to look at." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The link the chapter gives for preprocessing with OpenCV, autbor.com/preprocessingocr, opens a page titled Image Preprocessing for Tesseract OCR that calls itself a placeholder blog post and lists OpenCV functions for binarising, inverting, removing noise with a median blur, adding a border and finding the skew angle; OpenCV's own site answered this study with an access check, and OpenCV is not installed, so preprocessing is shown here with Pillow and with small grids of pixel values. The address autbor.com/embeddedfrankenstein that the practice program names opens a page titled Frankenstein." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P15, chx:P16, chx:P05 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For an advanced course, chapter 22 is best taught as a pipeline in which each step has a check: prepare the image (border, upright, contrast), choose the language and confirm the pack is installed, read the text, and then test the result with what a program can test (patterns for numbers, differences against a correction) before a person reviews it; the chapter's programs are correct for the tools they name, and what it leaves for the course to add is the page mode, the border and slant limits, the silent missing pack, the result and encoding checks around subprocess and open, and the plain statement that the book's images, NAPS2 and the language model were not reproduced here." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P04, chx:P05, chx:P07, chx:P11, chx:P12 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "17 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "16 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-01T12:00:00; 14 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
